From: mkxp-z-vita
Subject: [PATCH 0015] cont.c/thread_pthread.c: Vita fiber stacks inside the running thread's registered stack

WHY
---
A Vita thread registers a stack with the kernel (sceKernelCreateThread;
SceKernelThreadInfo.stack/.stackSize reports it back). A thread that enters a
syscall while its SP is outside that range is stopped with 0x10006 -- no
signal, no Ruby exception, the thread simply stops running.

The first fiber patch gave this port fiber machine stacks from malloc, which puts
every Fiber's SP on the libc heap. RGSS3 runs every event interpreter and the
message window inside a Fiber, and the first thing such a fiber does that
reaches the filesystem -- File.open, or Bitmap#draw_text, whose glyph load is
an FT_Stream_Seek + read -- stops the rgss thread. Hardware evidence
(three different player builds, byte-identical each time): the
rgss thread stopped at SceLibKernel seg1+0x69d4 with R0 = a file UID and
R2:R3 = 0x1a8a8:0, i.e. sceIoLseek(fd, 108712, 0), SP = 0x84d71a68 while its
THREAD_INFO said stack 0x8b201000 + 8 MiB.

WHAT
----
cont.c
  * fiber_pool_allocate_memory asks vita/ruby/vita_fiber_arena.c first. That
    module carves ONE arena out of the low end of the current thread's own
    registered stack and returns it with the number of stacks that fit. Every
    later request returns NULL, which fiber_pool_expand already turns into a
    FiberError -- deliberately, because the only other way to satisfy it is
    malloc, which is the bug. If the thread's stack is too small for an arena
    the module says so (one loud log line) and the stock malloc path runs.
  * fiber_pool_stack_acquire refuses to run a fiber on a thread that does not
    own the arena: its stacks are inside one specific thread's stack. Stacks
    are acquired at the first resume, so this is exactly where a fiber both
    created and resumed on a foreign thread arrives; Ruby's own "fiber called
    across threads" already covers the rest. A FiberError, not a 0x10006.
  * fiber_pool_stack_release never frees an arena-backed allocation. It is a
    slice of a live thread's stack, not a malloc block, and keeping its
    vacancies is what lets short-lived fibers recycle slots forever.

thread_pthread.c
  * ruby_init_stack gives Ruby the real floor of the thread's own stack. Vita
    has neither getrlimit(RLIMIT_STACK) nor pthread_getattr_np, so stock code
    leaves native_main_thread.stack_maxsize at 0; gc.c's stack_check() then
    evaluates STACK_LEVEL_MAX - 2048 as SIZE_MAX-2047 and never fires, so a
    runaway main-context recursion raises nothing and walks off the bottom of
    the stack. That was survivable while nothing lived down there. With the
    arena down there it would shred suspended fibers, so the floor is now the
    top of the arena plus a 128 KiB guard and an overflow is a SystemStackError
    long before it reaches a fiber.

Only __vita__ changes; off Vita every hunk preprocesses to stock MRI.
The -I that resolves <vita_fiber_arena.h> is injected into XCFLAGS by
vita/scripts/build-ruby-vita.sh, which also compiles the module into
libruby-vita.a.

--- a/cont.c
+++ b/cont.c
@@ -44,6 +44,19 @@
 #define RB_PAGE_SIZE (pagesize)
 #define RB_PAGE_MASK (~(RB_PAGE_SIZE - 1))
 static long pagesize;
+
+#ifdef __vita__
+/* a Vita thread may only enter a syscall while its SP is inside
+ * the stack it registered with the kernel; one caught outside is stopped with
+ * 0x10006 at that syscall, deterministically and uncatchably. Fiber machine
+ * stacks therefore cannot come from malloc: the first
+ * File, Bitmap#draw_text (glyph load = seek + read) or sleep inside a Fiber
+ * kills the thread -- and RGSS3 runs every event interpreter and the message
+ * window inside a Fiber. vita/ruby/vita_fiber_arena.c carves the pool's
+ * backing memory out of the low end of that same thread stack instead.
+ * The -I for this header is injected by vita/scripts/build-ruby-vita.sh. */
+#include <vita_fiber_arena.h>
+#endif
 
 static const rb_data_type_t cont_data_type, fiber_data_type;
 static VALUE rb_cContinuation;
@@ -417,6 +430,22 @@
 inline static void *
 fiber_pool_allocate_memory(size_t * count, size_t stride)
 {
+#ifdef __vita__
+    /* One allocation, covering the whole arena, never freed. `count` comes
+     * back as everything that fits, which fiber_pool_expand then turns into
+     * that many vacancies. Any later request has to FAIL: satisfying it could
+     * only mean malloc, and a fiber running on malloc'd memory is exactly the
+     * 0x10006 stop this port is fixing. fiber_pool_expand turns the NULL into
+     * a FiberError, which is a Ruby exception, not a crash. */
+    {
+        void *arena = NULL;
+        int rc = vita_fiber_arena_acquire(&arena, count, stride);
+
+        if (rc == VITA_FIBER_ARENA_OK) return arena;
+        if (rc != VITA_FIBER_ARENA_FALLBACK) return NULL;
+    }
+#endif
+
     // We use a divide-by-2 strategy to try and allocate memory. We are trying
     // to allocate `count` stacks. In normal situation, this won't fail. But
     // if we ran out of address space, or we are allocating more memory than
@@ -626,8 +655,24 @@
 static struct fiber_pool_stack
 fiber_pool_stack_acquire(struct fiber_pool * fiber_pool)
 {
-    struct fiber_pool_vacancy * vacancy = fiber_pool_vacancy_pop(fiber_pool);
+    struct fiber_pool_vacancy * vacancy;
 
+#ifdef __vita__
+    /* Every stack in the pool lives inside ONE thread's registered stack, so
+     * a fiber running on any other thread would be right back outside it.
+     * Stacks are acquired at the first resume (fiber_prepare_stack), which is
+     * where a fiber both created and resumed on a foreign thread arrives;
+     * Ruby's own "fiber called across threads" catches the rest. Either way
+     * the answer is a Ruby exception rather than a 0x10006. */
+    if (vita_fiber_arena_active() && !vita_fiber_arena_owns_current_thread()) {
+        rb_raise(rb_eFiberError,
+                 "can't run a Fiber on this thread: the Vita fiber stack "
+                 "arena belongs to the thread that started Ruby");
+    }
+#endif
+
+    vacancy = fiber_pool_vacancy_pop(fiber_pool);
+
     if (DEBUG) fprintf(stderr, "fiber_pool_stack_acquire: %p used=%"PRIuSIZE"\n", (void*)fiber_pool->vacancies, fiber_pool->used);
 
     if (!vacancy) {
@@ -733,11 +778,21 @@
 
 #ifdef FIBER_POOL_ALLOCATION_FREE
     struct fiber_pool_allocation * allocation = stack->allocation;
+
+#ifdef __vita__
+    /* The arena is a slice of a live thread's stack. Handing it back would
+     * unhook every vacancy and then free() a pointer malloc never returned;
+     * it stays for the life of the process, and the vacancies with it, which
+     * is what lets short-lived fibers recycle slots forever. */
+    const int arena_backed = vita_fiber_arena_contains(allocation->base);
+#else
+    const int arena_backed = 0;
+#endif
 
     allocation->used -= 1;
 
     // Release address space and/or dirty memory:
-    if (allocation->used == 0) {
+    if (allocation->used == 0 && !arena_backed) {
         fiber_pool_allocation_free(allocation);
     }
     else if (stack->pool->free_stacks) {
--- a/thread_pthread.c
+++ b/thread_pthread.c
@@ -14,6 +14,13 @@
 #include "gc.h"
 #include "mjit.h"
 
+#ifdef __vita__
+/* the fiber arena sits at the bottom of this thread's stack, so
+ * ruby_init_stack has to know where the thread's own stack really ends.
+ * The -I is injected by vita/scripts/build-ruby-vita.sh. */
+#include <vita_fiber_arena.h>
+#endif
+
 #ifdef HAVE_SYS_RESOURCE_H
 #include <sys/resource.h>
 #endif
@@ -939,6 +946,26 @@
 {
     native_main_thread.id = pthread_self();
 
+#ifdef __vita__
+    /* Vita has neither getrlimit(RLIMIT_STACK) nor pthread_getattr_np, so
+     * MAINSTACKADDR_AVAILABLE is 0 and the HAVE_GETRLIMIT block below is
+     * compiled out: stack_maxsize stays 0. gc.c's stack_check() then computes
+     * maximum_length = STACK_LEVEL_MAX - 2048 == SIZE_MAX-2047 and never
+     * fires, so a runaway recursion on this thread raises nothing and simply
+     * walks off the bottom of the stack. With the fiber arena down there
+     * that would silently shred suspended fibers, so tell
+     * Ruby where the thread's own stack really ends. */
+    {
+        uintptr_t floor = vita_fiber_arena_main_stack_floor();
+
+        if (floor != 0 && (uintptr_t)addr > floor) {
+            native_main_thread.stack_start = (VALUE *)addr;
+            native_main_thread.stack_maxsize = (size_t)((uintptr_t)addr - floor);
+            return;
+        }
+    }
+#endif
+
 #if MAINSTACKADDR_AVAILABLE
     if (native_main_thread.stack_maxsize) return;
     {
